I’ll inspect the diffraction data and metadata first, then identify candidate phases, build/refine the multiphase model in GSAS-II, and validate that the exported CIFs, profile, fractions, and project all come from the same final refinement.

The dominant pattern is already consistent with wolframite-type ZnWO₄, the expected 900 °C reaction product. I’m now checking every unmatched reflection against residual ZnO/WO₃ and other zinc-tungstate polymorphs before deciding whether the data support one phase or a minor secondary phase.

The initial refinements confirm ZnWO₄ as dominant and show that a Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ model fits slightly better than Kα₁ alone. The first multiphase trials also expose a real WO₃-like contribution, but one database model has a metrically singular monoclinic cell and destabilizes cell refinement; I’m replacing it with physically appropriate WO₃ variants and fixing a redundant GSAS-II scale parameter before quantitative comparison.

The corrected comparison is running through one-, two-, and three-phase refinements with identical background and profile treatment. The decisive test is whether adding WO₃ or ZnO yields a positive, stable scale and removes phase-specific residual peaks—not merely a lower global R factor.

The three-phase hypothesis is strongly supported: ZnO produces a stable ~7 wt% contribution with a refined cell close to zincite and cuts Rwp further after WO₃ is included. I’m now testing a physically constrained peak-width model to make sure that fraction is not an artifact of flexible empirical broadening.

The constrained-width check keeps the phase fractions within about 1 wt% absolute of the best profile fit, so the three phases are robust. I’ll use the better-fitting final refinement, while reporting the fraction uncertainty conservatively to reflect the uncalibrated instrumental profile rather than relying only on very small formal least-squares errors.

